ID: B3-Ch12a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_approach,0)
set(v_b3_ch12_believe,0)
set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,0)
set(v_b3_ch12_invest,0)
set(v_b3_ch12_spell,0)
set(v_is_dead,0)
 

"Arraka, wake up," Illuna says. "Barry finished your particle drill ten minutes ago. If you leave him idle, he goes right back to that blinding flash, and none of us want another demonstration."

"Hey," I say. "You will thank me for every repetition the day a fight goes sideways. That flash has a half-second window. If my timing slips, I blind our own frontline."

"Fascinating," Illuna says, flat, and lifts the gold amulet off Flower's collarbone. "Arraka. Answer, or I start spinning the chain."

"Alright, alright," Arraka snaps from the metal. "I will give the boy his next drill. Give me a minute. I am working."

"On what?" Illuna asks. "A smaller explosion radius so you can catch us off guard on your next escape attempt?"

"Hardly. If there were a cheaper blast formula, I would have blown this trinket open three years ago. I am adjusting my soul-absorption matrix. A variant that merges instead of consuming, leaving equal control over the vessel."

Illuna stares at the gold piece. "Like Flower and me? Why would you do that on purpose? You spent a decade complaining about Therius."

"Therius was an accident," Arraka says. "And if I take Flower's body by force again, both of you die, the troupe attacks me out of loyalty, and I have to slaughter every one of them. For good, this time."

"And that bothers you?" Illuna's voice stays cold. "You killed us once without blinking."

"Because dead trainees cannot butcher gods for me!" Arraka's voice rattles the casing. "Where else am I finding a collection of armed misfits with personal grievances against the heavens and enough raw luck to survive my lessons? You think I want to go back to solo revenge after designing something this entertaining?"

Illuna's brow furrows. "You expect me to believe you would share a skull with Flower and me just to keep your vanguard alive?"

"Why not?" Arraka says cheerfully. "Morning tea and biscuits with the little girl, evening massacres with the rest of you. We can make a schedule."

"I find that impossible to believe," Illuna says.

"Good," Arraka says. "If you trusted a spirit who already murdered you once, I would have to disown you as a student."

choice("Tell Arraka that I believe her.", B3-Ch12a-Combining, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Combining, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch12_believe = 1)
choice("Tell Arraka that I don't believe her either.", B3-Ch12a-Combining, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Combining, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch12_believe = 0)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Combining
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_believe == 1) {
"Well, I believe her," I say.

"Listen to that," Arraka tells Illuna. "Barry is gullible to a fault. A few polite days and he thinks I am harmless. Be glad you have more sense than he does."

"On that single point," Illuna says dryly, "we agree."

"Right. Very funny," I say, stepping back. "I will be by the boulder line running combinations."

I leave Arraka to her notes and head toward the edge of the clearing. For two days since leaving the lessathi complex, every spare hour has gone into the notebook—indexing every spell now available through the booster, tracing flows, testing limits.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_believe == 0) {
"For what it's worth, I don't believe her either," I say.

"Sensible," Arraka says. "Perhaps you have a survival instinct after all. Now go play with your spells while I finish here."

"I was already on my way," I say. "I will be by the rocks."

I leave Arraka to her notes and head toward the edge of the clearing. For two days since leaving the lessathi complex, every spare hour has gone into the notebook—indexing every spell now available through the booster, tracing flows, testing limits.

}
Layering spells is another matter entirely. Back on the Western Continent, Brinhorn and I spent months theorizing combinations on parchment, but paper never pushes back against conflicting mana channels. The stat device fires spells in discrete bursts; weaving two together means manual intervention—guiding the threads by hand just like an ordinary caster. Brinhorn made it look effortless, tweaking particle flows on the fly in his endless race to match Azarius. Out here, with only level-one access, every weave demands trial and error.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Practicing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Practicing, v_available_points = +4, v_available_points_aux = +4)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Practicing
TEXT:

"At it again, Barry?" Hadrik ambles past, an open clay bottle in one hand and a broad grin under his beard. "Need a moving target? I have ten minutes before dinner and just enough dwarven ale in me to ignore the risks."

"Actually, yes," I say, closing the notebook. "I have targeted utility spells that only take hold on living flesh. Daren and Kate refused to let me near them all morning."

"Can't blame them!" Hadrik chuckles, setting the bottle down on a flat stump.

He backs off ten paces, spreading his arms wide with an exaggerated bow.

"Whenever you're ready, lad. Hit me with everything you've got."

I raise a hand, channeling the flow—and Hadrik blurs sideways, kicking up dirt.

"Too slow, Barry! Far too slow!"

"I thought you were volunteering to help," I call out.

"This is help!" Hadrik bellows, circling at speed. "Enemies don't plant their boots and wait for your incantation. Moving targets build character. Besides, it's more fun this way."

"Fine," I mutter, resetting my stance.

Before I can lock on, the booster in my coat pocket gives four short, rhythmic chirps. New points registered.

Perfect timing.

choice("Invest points now", B3-Ch12a-Bored, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Bored, v_b3_ch12_invest = 1, special:stats)
choice("Invest points later(by pressing the stats button on the top of the screen)", B3-Ch12a-Bored, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Bored, v_b3_ch12_invest = 0)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Bored
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_invest == 1) {
"Hold on," Hadrik calls out, skidding to a halt. "Are we sparring or staring?"

"One second—allocating points," I say, tapping the display and tucking the device back in. "Ready now."

I need to land a cast before Hadrik loses interest and walks off.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_invest == 0) {
I mute the device with a quick tap through my coat and keep my eyes fixed on the dwarf. Hadrik weaves left and right in short, mocking bursts, daring me to commit.

I need to land a cast before he loses interest and walks off.

}
Arraka mentioned that Daren reads Hadrik's movements by tracking the shifts in his aura. Without Arraka's specialized training, parsing those currents is tricky, but raw magical sense might bridge the gap.

If not, sheer physical agility could close the distance before he pivots.

Or I can just lie to him.

choice("Try to predict Hadrik's movements from his aura.", B3-Ch12a-Predict, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Predict, v_b3_ch12_approach = 0)
choice("Attempt to get close to Hadrik so I can aim at him easier.", B3-Ch12a-Brute, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Brute, v_b3_ch12_approach = 1)
choice("Tell Hadrik that him standing in place would make it harder for me to aim at him.", B3-Ch12a-Bluff, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Bluff, v_b3_ch12_approach = 2)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Predict
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,0) if (v_magical_sense == 4 && v_perception < 4 || v_magical_sense < 4)
set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,1) if (v_magical_sense == 4 && v_perception == 4)
#if(v_magical_sense < 4) {
I fix my gaze on Hadrik, trying to parse the shifts in his aura as he bounds across the clearing. The energy around him is a dense, shifting blur. There is supposed to be a tell before every shift in momentum, but to my untrained perception, it is just noise.

Thirty seconds pass with me squinting across empty dirt while the dwarf darts between the pines. He finally slows, shaking his head with a grin, and scoops his clay bottle off the stump.

"Minute's up, Barry," Hadrik calls out. "Put some elbow grease into it next time, aye?"

"Wait—let me get one cast off!" I say.

"Later, lad," Hadrik chuckles, heading toward the tents. "Dinner calls."

I let out a breath, pull out my notebook, and return to cataloguing spell formulas.

}
#if(v_magical_sense == 4 && v_perception < 4) {
I focus on the currents around Hadrik's frame. There is a distinct flare in his aura an instant before he pivots, pulling tight along his flanks. Logically, the direction of that pull should forecast his vector, but the shifts come too fast for me to calculate a lead angle.

For thirty seconds I track the flares, hesitating each time he cuts across the grass. Before I can commit to a trajectory, Hadrik slows to a walk and picks up his bottle.

"Minute's up, Barry," Hadrik says. "Put some elbow grease into it next time, aye?"

"Wait—give me one more try!" I say.

"Some other time, lad," Hadrik laughs, turning back toward camp. "My throat is dry."

I let out a breath, pull out my notebook, and return to cataloguing spell formulas.

}
#if(v_magical_sense == 4 && v_perception == 4) { His dwarf form holds a massive reservoir of energy, but just before he breaks into a sprint, the aura around the fingertips of his left hand thins, trailing like smoke in the exact direction he is about to move. The speed of that pull corresponds to how hard he intends to push off.

It is a split-second window, but it is a tell.

I select the lightest targeted cantrip in the booster—a minor disorientation pulse that induces a fleeting, mild dizziness. I lead him by two paces and release. The timing is off by half a beat; the spell discharges into empty air.

I adjust on the next pass. Hadrik cuts left, his aura trailing toward the riverbank. I lead him three paces out, catch his rhythm, and release on the third stride.

The pulse connects.

Hadrik stumbles mid-dash, skids two yards through the dirt, and claps a thick hand to his forehead, blinking rapidly.

"Whoa..." Hadrik rubs his temples, looking around. "Was that you? A dizzy spell? Ha—already gone."

"It is the weakest disorientation formula available," I say. "Barely a head rush. But the targeting locked."

"Well, glad to be of service!" Hadrik says, grinning as he retrieves his bottle from the stump.

"Leaving already?" I ask.

"That's enough tumbling for one evening," he says, taking a swig. "If you need more live targets tomorrow, we'll find an orc patrol. Tyrath's raiders aren't exactly hiding."

"True enough," I say.

"See you by the fire, Barry."

I pull out my notebook to record the lead timing.

}
Ten minutes later, Arraka's voice calls across the grass.

"Alright, Barry-boy. Let us see the fruits of your labor. Put a fire arrow into that oak over there."

"That won't work," I tell her. "I know the theory, but material casting doesn't follow ethereal rules."

"Did you or did you not construct spells on the ethereal plane by picturing every individual particle and guiding their paths by force of will?"

"Yes, but that plane is—"

"A simple yes will do," Arraka snaps. "Is there any basic projectile with a simpler flow than a fire arrow?"

"No, but—"

"Then shut up and cast it. Channel the flow exactly as you did in the ether and it will catch."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Hermit, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Hermit)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Brute
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,0) if (v_agility == 4 && v_reflexes < 4 || v_agility < 4)
set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,1) if (v_agility == 4 && v_reflexes == 4)
#if(v_agility < 4) {
Instead of trying to outthink him, I dig my boots into the turf and sprint straight at him the moment he pauses. I prime the dizziness cantrip, aiming to close the gap before releasing.

Hadrik does not even brace. The instant I enter casting range, he slips past my shoulder like a phantom, leaving me lunging into open grass.

I turn and chase him down twice more, but the dwarf's burst speed is absurd. My lungs burn before I can even line up a proper sightline.

"Well... good hustle, lad!" Hadrik laughs, strolling back to the stump while I catch my breath with hands on my knees.

"Wait," I wheeze. "One more pass."

"Minute's up, Barry," Hadrik says cheerfully, uncorking his bottle. "Better luck next round."

I shake my head, retrieve my notebook from the grass, and go back to mapping spell flows.

}
#if(v_agility == 4 && v_reflexes < 4) {
I dig my heels in and burst forward the moment Hadrik hesitates. With four points in agility, I match his pace across the first ten yards, closing the distance in a fraction of a second.

Hadrik blinks, surprised by the sudden charge, but before my hands can complete the casting gesture, he drops low and cuts behind a pine.

For the next half-minute I stay on his heels, cornering him twice near the riverbank, but my reflexes lag behind my sprint. Every time I raise my hand to release the spell, he slips off the axis of my palm.

"Quite the chase!" Hadrik chuckles, leaning against a boulder with his bottle in hand while I catch my breath. "Thought you had me on that second turn!"

"Hold on," I say. "Give me ten more seconds."

"Time's up, lad," Hadrik says, taking a hearty drink. "Pace yourself."

I sheath my frustration, open the notebook, and return to spell analysis.

}
#if(v_agility == 4 && v_reflexes == 4) {
I dig my boots in and sprint straight at him. The physical boost carries me across the clearing in an instant, cutting off his angle before he can gather momentum.

Hadrik reacts, but my reflexes match the opening. As he tries to duck under my left arm, I pivot on the ball of my foot, lock the gesture into his chest, and release the dizziness cantrip at point-blank range.

The spell catches.

Hadrik stumbles, shakes his head vigorously, and braces a hand against his knee, blinking away the fog.

"Whoa..." Hadrik says, straightening up. "Was that you? A dizzy spell? Ha—cleared right up!"

"It is the weakest variant in the index," I say. "Only meant to disrupt focus for a heartbeat. But it connected."

"Glad I could help, then!" Hadrik says, grinning as he walks back to grab his bottle.

"Leaving already?" I ask.

"That's enough tumbling for one evening," he says, taking a swig. "If you need more live targets tomorrow, we'll find an orc patrol. Tyrath's raiders aren't exactly hiding."

"True enough," I say.

"See you by the fire, Barry."

I pull out my notebook to record the timing.

}
Ten minutes later, Arraka's voice calls across the grass.

"Alright, Barry-boy. Let us see the fruits of your labor. Put a fire arrow into that oak over there."

"That won't work," I tell her. "I know the theory, but material casting doesn't follow ethereal rules."

"Did you or did you not construct spells on the ethereal plane by picturing every individual particle and guiding their paths by force of will?"

"Yes, but that plane is—"

"A simple yes will do," Arraka snaps. "Is there any basic projectile with a simpler flow than a fire arrow?"

"No, but—"

"Then shut up and cast it. Channel the flow exactly as you did in the ether and it will catch."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Hermit, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Hermit)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Bluff
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,0) if (v_bluff < 4)
set(v_b3_ch12_hadrik,1) if (v_bluff == 4)
#if(v_bluff < 4) {
"Hey, Hadrik," I call out. "You do realize that him standing in place would make it harder for me to aim at him, right?"

"Hah!" Hadrik bellows, bounding from rock to rock. "Nice try, Barry! There isn't a dwarf in the Northern Kingdom who falls for that one!"

"Worth a shot," I mutter, keeping my hands raised as he circles.

After another thirty seconds of chasing his trail with my palms, Hadrik slows down and picks up his bottle.

"Well... points for effort!" Hadrik says, taking a swig.

"Wait, let me cast at least one!" I say.

"Some other time, lad," Hadrik says, wandering off toward camp.

I let out a sigh, pull out my notebook, and get back to cataloguing formulas.

}
#if(v_bluff == 4) {
"Hey, Hadrik," I say with absolute gravity. "You do realize that him standing in place would make it harder for me to aim at him, right?"

"Hah!" Hadrik laughs, darting sideways. "Nice try, Barry, but—wait. If I stop moving, you have no velocity to lead against..."

"Exactly," I say, and the moment his boots hit the grass, I snap the dizziness cantrip into his chest.

Hadrik blinks, swaying slightly, and presses a hand to his temple.

"I don't believe it," Hadrik grunts as the fog clears. "I know you have a bluff stat in that gadget and I still walked into it. Well, that won't work twice. Go on—tell me to stop again."

"I would never reuse the same angle," I say smoothly. "Though I did read that holding that exact stance for sixty seconds doubles your aura density."

"Right," Hadrik snorts, darting away as I raise my hands. "Fascinating lore, lad. Tell me more!"

"The woodland spirits placed a ward on this glade," I call after him. "Excessive running rots your eyesight."

"Splendid!" Hadrik shouts over his shoulder, grinning. "Eiden fights with his eyes shut anyway. Level playing field!"

"Stop blabbering, you fool!" Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet across camp. "A bluff requires novelty! He knows you are lying! Stop making a circus out of my glade and cast your spells!"

"Well, you heard the banshee," Hadrik says, picking up his bottle. "Enough nonsense for one night."

"At least let me test one more," I say.

"Tomorrow, Barry," Hadrik says, wandering off toward the fire.

I sit down against a boulder, reopen the notebook, and resume indexing.

}
Ten minutes later, Arraka's voice calls across the grass.

"Alright, Barry-boy. Let us see the fruits of your labor. Put a fire arrow into that oak over there."

"That won't work," I tell her. "I know the theory, but material casting doesn't follow ethereal rules."

"Did you or did you not construct spells on the ethereal plane by picturing every individual particle and guiding their paths by force of will?"

"Yes, but that plane is—"

"A simple yes will do," Arraka snaps. "Is there any basic projectile with a simpler flow than a fire arrow?"

"No, but—"

"Then shut up and cast it. Channel the flow exactly as you did in the ether and it will catch."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Hermit, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Hermit)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Hermit
TEXT:

"Fine," I say, stepping into the center of the glade. "You're the teacher."

I set my feet, raise both palms toward the gnarled oak, and draw on the raw trickle from the magical plane. The particles scatter immediately, resisting the channel, but I don't try to wrestle them into alignment. In the ether, forcing an errant flow only breaks the lattice. Instead, I track the drift of the stray sparks with my fingertips, matching their cadence until the turbulence smooths out and the particles fall into a tight, spinning braid.

"Curious," Arraka's voice drifts from the amulet. "Most novices try to bend the flow by main force. Where did you pick up that dampening trick?"

"An old hermit in the Western mountains," I say, keeping my breathing steady. "Never caught his name. Might not have offered one."

"A hermit with particle discipline?" Arraka scoffs. "Criminal waste of talent. If I ever shatter this gold shell, I will track him down and make him an apprentice. Remind me to take his description before I kill you."

"I'll write it down," I mutter. My palms tingle as the braid catches fire. "Can I release this, or do I—"

A needle-thin dart of red flame snaps off my right index finger. It streaks ten yards across the grass, strikes the oak with a dry pop, and scorches a black ring the size of a copper coin into the rough bark before flickering out.

I stare at the singed wood.

"It worked," I breathe, a grin spreading across my face. "It actually worked. You're a genius."

"Naturally I am a genius," Arraka purrs. "Did you expect amateur instruction from someone who dismantled half the pantheon?"

"I take back every complaint," I say. "That was completely unassisted."

"Unbelievable," Illuna says, crossing her arms as she steps out of the shadow of the tent. "You two are celebrating a spark that failed to set dry bark on fire. Two days of Arraka's lunacy, and the grand result is a smudge. How can you not see she is amusing herself at your expense?"

"Every exercise had sound theory behind it," I tell her.

"You spent forty minutes striking your forehead against that trunk!" Illuna's voice rises. "Are you telling me self-concussion is part of the curriculum?"

"Of course not," I say.

"Then why do it?"

"Because he understands the fundamentals," Arraka cuts in. "He is the only one here with enough general theory to follow instructions without whining. He knew the practical execution looked ridiculous, but the underlying resonance principle was sound. Imprinting physical aura on an inanimate target lowers the barrier for weak channels."

"Exactly," I say. "It's an obscure drill for casters with stunted output. You saturate the target's surface so the projectile has something familiar to home in on."

"And why bother when you have a functioning device in your coat?" Illuna demands. "Why build from scratch?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Exercise, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Exercise)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Exercise
TEXT:

"Because fire arrow is not in his index," Arraka says. "That was the entire point of the lesson. The booster was manufactured six centuries ago; the fire arrow formula was codified five hundred years ago. Do you grasp what that means? Until ten minutes ago, Barry assumed he was chained to the machine's catalog. Now he knows he can draw directly from the plane. With sufficient drill, he can learn my personal spellbook. Primordial matrices, even. The ceiling is gone."

Illuna looks between me and the gold amulet, her cold expression faltering. "You two are planning that far ahead?"

"We intend to kill a god, don't we?" I say.

"Yes," Illuna says softly. "I suppose we do. And the fact that it sounds less insane every day makes me question my own mind."

"Hah!" Arraka cackles. "Stick to my regimen, little elf. By next month, regicide will feel like routine maintenance."

"Hadrik!" I call out as the dwarf ambles by. "Pour a cup of ale! First unaided fire cast. Look at that oak—right in the bark!"

"Good on you, lad!" Hadrik laughs, clapping a heavy hand between my shoulder blades. "Master of the arcane!"

Diane walks over from the riverbank, followed by Daren and Kate, drawn by the noise. It takes five minutes to explain why a singed oak trunk matters, and why knocking my forehead against it was legitimate study, but once the logic lands, we settle in a loose circle near the firepit for a brief rest.

"So, Barry," Flower says, leaning forward with bright green eyes. "Now that you can cast anything, what's next? A giant fire dragon? With wings and claws that blow things up?"

She reaches absentmindedly toward an open bottle on the grass, but her eyes flash blue for half a second and her left hand sharply slaps her right hand away.

"He lacks the reservoir for a construct," Illuna says from within. "Free casting does not grant infinite mana. His output is still level one."

"Precisely," Arraka says. "Which is why my proprietary matrices are superior. My formulas scale dynamically with whatever fuel you feed them. Pure efficiency."

"You sound like a street peddler pushing snake oil," Illuna retorts. "The fire arrow scaled on the exact same curve."

"A modern fluke," Arraka sneers. "Show me an ancient school that matched my density ratios."

"Regardless," I say, steering the conversation back. "I need low-cost formulas to build muscle memory before attempting anything complex. Maybe the toad conjuration from the beginner's summoning primer. Or that hovering dagger trick stage illusionists use. Or sparklers for night signaling."

"Or," Arraka sighs, "you could pick something that isn't entirely useless."

choice("Decide to try out the frog summoning spell first.", B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_b3_ch12_spell = 0)
choice("Decide to try out the floating dagger spell first.", B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_b3_ch12_spell = 1)
choice("Decide to try out the flying sparks spell first.", B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_b3_ch12_spell = 2)
choice("Decide to try out a spell that isn't useless.", B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Flash, v_b3_ch12_spell = 3)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Flash
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 0) {
"Decided," I say, ignoring the amulet. "The toad summoning comes first."

"Splendid," Arraka mutters. "Ignore the ancient banshee. What could she possibly know about combat magic?"

"Are we getting a demonstration, Barry?" Diane asks with a warm smile. "No better stage than an attentive audience."

"It needs a few hours of ether simulation first," I say. "Though if you want live casting, I can always run the blinding flash again—"

"NO!" Daren, Kate, and Illuna shout in unison.

"Not that blasted flash," Kate groans, rubbing her brow. "We've had enough spots in our eyes for one afternoon."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 1) {
"Decided," I say, ignoring the amulet. "The floating dagger comes first."

"Splendid," Arraka mutters. "Ignore the ancient banshee. What could she possibly know about combat magic?"

"Are we getting a demonstration, Barry?" Diane asks with a warm smile. "No better stage than an attentive audience."

"It needs a few hours of ether simulation first," I say. "Though if you want live casting, I can always run the blinding flash again—"

"NO!" Daren, Kate, and Illuna shout in unison.

"Not that blasted flash," Kate groans, rubbing her brow. "We've had enough spots in our eyes for one afternoon."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 2) {
"Decided," I say, ignoring the amulet. "The sparkler cantrip comes first."

"Splendid," Arraka mutters. "Ignore the ancient banshee. What could she possibly know about combat magic?"

"Are we getting a demonstration, Barry?" Diane asks with a warm smile. "No better stage than an attentive audience."

"It needs a few hours of ether simulation first," I say. "Though if you want live casting, I can always run the blinding flash again—"

"NO!" Daren, Kate, and Illuna shout in unison.

"Not that blasted flash," Kate groans, rubbing her brow. "We've had enough spots in our eyes for one afternoon."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 3 && v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2) {
"You win, Arraka," I say. "I will work on a hard-light illusion of a pointed dunce cap fitted directly over your amulet."

"First the painted mustache, now millinery," Arraka says dryly. "The heights of your creativity continue to astound."

"I have plenty more where that came from," I say.

"I know a very deep ditch where you can deposit them," she snaps.

"Are we getting a demonstration, Barry?" Diane asks with a warm smile. "No better stage than an attentive audience."

"It needs a few hours of ether simulation first," I say. "Though if you want live casting, I can always run the blinding flash again—"

"NO!" Daren, Kate, and Illuna shout in unison.

"Not that blasted flash," Kate groans, rubbing her brow. "We've had enough spots in our eyes for one afternoon."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 3 && v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
"You win, Arraka," I say. "I will work on a hard-light illusion of a pointed dunce cap fitted directly over your amulet."

"Remarkable dedication to juvenile spite," Arraka says. "That optical lattice is three times more complex than standard basic illusions."

"If the geometry gets tricky, I can always spend two hours knocking my head against your casing," I say. "Resonance training. You understand."

"By all means," Arraka sneers. "I never turn down free self-harm."

"Are we getting a demonstration, Barry?" Diane asks with a warm smile. "No better stage than an attentive audience."

"It needs a few hours of ether simulation first," I say. "Though if you want live casting, I can always run the blinding flash again—"

"NO!" Daren, Kate, and Illuna shout in unison.

"Not that blasted flash," Kate groans, rubbing her brow. "We've had enough spots in our eyes for one afternoon."

}
"Look, I know it's grating," I tell them. "But the reflex has to be instinct. When I yell 'Daren, catch!' you drop your eyelids for exactly one beat, no matter what is swinging at you. It is an archaic disruption spell; modern duelists don't train against it. We get one chance to blind an enemy line before they adapt."

"Barry, lad," Hadrik rumbles, taking a long pull from his bottle. "We're happy for you, truly. But you've talked about mana flows for forty-eight hours straight. Give the magic a rest for an hour and drink like a mortal."

"Leave him be, Hadrik," Daren says with a chuckle. "The man chased this since childhood. Let him enjoy the breakthrough. Though I agree on the flash—my retinas need a ceasefire."

"What else did you test today, Barry?" Diane asks. "Any other discoveries?"

#if(v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0) {
"Mostly combinations and the tree resonance," I say. "I tried to land a mild dizziness pulse on Hadrik, but he was moving too fast for me to lead the target."

"A disorientation spell at level one?" Kate asks, looking up.

"Barely a flicker of lightheadedness," I say. "Low-tier disruption."

"I did not expect Wolford's daze on a starter node," Kate says. "Most disorientation formulas require far denser channels."

"I used Wolford's matrix against Tyrath," I say. "Though whether my capacity ever reaches that peak again is anyone's guess."

"What is Wolford's daze?" Flower asks, leaning in. "Never heard of it."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1) {
"Mostly combinations and the tree resonance," I say. "I managed to tag Hadrik with a mild dizziness pulse, but he only gave me one clean pass before dinner called."

"A disorientation spell at level one?" Kate asks, looking up.

"Barely a flicker of lightheadedness," I say. "Low-tier disruption."

"I did not expect Wolford's daze on a starter node," Kate says. "Most disorientation formulas require far denser channels."

"I used Wolford's matrix against Tyrath," I say. "Though whether my capacity ever reaches that peak again is anyone's guess."

"What is Wolford's daze?" Flower asks, leaning in. "Never heard of it."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Wolford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Wolford)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Wolford
TEXT:

"Wolford's daze," Kate says. "It bypasses standard magical shielding entirely, but its anchor is fragile. If the target fixes their vision on three separate points within five seconds, the spell unravels. If they fail, the feedback produces an unbearable headache. Almost no one studies it because any disciplined duelist breaks the lock by glancing around."

"Speaking of targeted spells," I say, glancing around the circle, "it would help if I could run live tests on someone once or twice. Just to gauge resist thresholds before combat."

"No," Kate says immediately, her tone flat. "I know you mean well, Barry. But the Beacon cured me of any interest in being an experiment."

"Understood," I say, holding up a hand. "Forget I asked."

"Same here," Daren says with an apologetic shrug. "My defensive enchantments have fixed daily charges. Dropping them for testing wastes warding we might need tonight, and casting through them tells you nothing about baseline resistance."

"I could use the practice even against active wards, but fair enough," I say.

"I can do it, Barry!" Diane speaks up, leaning forward with a bright smile. "I'm happy to help."

I look at her, hesitant. "Are you sure? You went through the Beacon as well. I don't want to push."

"Ancient history," Diane says breezily. "It doesn't bother me in the least. Test whatever you need."

"If you're certain, I'd appreciate it," I say.

"Any time."

"Enough social hour," Arraka snaps from the amulet. "Kate. You have been running aura disruption exercises behind the baggage cart for two days. Are you going to demonstrate, or is it merely decorative?"

Kate stiffens slightly. "I wanted cleaner consistency before showing the group."

"You were doing fine," Arraka says. "The problem is you need a live current to practice cutting. Daren, float your blade. Let her show what she took from my lectures."

Daren draws his broadsword and releases his grip. The blade hangs at chest height, suspended on a faint shimmer of white aura. "Now what?"

"Kate," Arraka says. "Cut the thread."

Kate raises two fingers. A thin sliver of ice materializes in front of her palm, coated in a dark, needle-thin aura. She flicks her wrist; the shard zips between Daren and the hilt.

The white shimmer snaps. The sword drops flat into the dirt with a heavy thud.

"On the first pass," Daren says, impressed, scooping the weapon up. "Clean work."

"She listens," Arraka says smugly. "Unlike the rest of you, she pieced the technique together just by eavesdropping on Daren's remedial lessons. Now—one final check. Kate, take Heldenbusorag's scepter and put a maximum-yield freeze beam into that granite slab across the creek. Max output. Burn the channel."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Scepter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Scepter)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Scepter
TEXT:

"I am not draining my reservoir for target practice," Kate says, frowning at the scepter in her lap. "If an ambush hits us while I'm empty—"

"Your passive recovery is faster than anyone here," Arraka cuts in. "Lie on the grass for five minutes afterward and you are combat-ready. Stop stalling. This is critical."

Kate exhales through her teeth, picks up the scepter, and rises. "One shot."

"Compress the gathering phase," Arraka adds. "Give the target zero time to evade."

"That rock looks terrified," Hadrik grunts into his ale.

Kate sets her stance and points the silver tip at the granite boulder across the water. Her aura flares—dense, cold, pulling inward in a violent spiral.

In less than two seconds, a lance of pure frost erupts from the scepter.

The blast hits with the force of a battering ram, echoing off the trees with a deafening crack. Frost blankets the creek in an instant; the granite boulder shatters down its center, encased in six inches of solid blue ice. The clearing's temperature drops twenty degrees in a heartbeat, breath pluming white from our mouths.

From the amulet, three voices laugh at once in high, triumphant harmony.

"Aha-hahahaha!" Arraka roars. "I knew the relic would deliver! Do you understand what we have? That is our counter for my past self! I know every ward I had active during that era. Caught off guard, my younger self cannot absorb a frost lance of that density. Two seconds of distraction from you clowns, Kate freezes the shell solid, and Daren finishes the job!"

"During the ritual," Illuna says, "your past self deflected Tyrath's prime dragon breath. Why would this penetrate where dragon fire failed?"

"It didn't deflect," Arraka snaps. "It pinned her attention long enough for Tyrath to teleport you away. Kate's beam will lock the copy in place just long enough for Daren's blade."

"And the sword bypasses her wards?" Kate asks, lowering the frost-coated scepter.

"Daren's blade is saturated with anti-magic enchantments," Arraka says. "Passive shields crumble on contact. Encased in ice, she has no time to weave an active barrier. She dies on the first cut."

choice(""Will we be able to use the same strategy against the God of Time?"", B3-Ch12a-Manner, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Manner, v_b3_ch12_chance = 0)
choice(""So, I'm guessing that we will only have one chance to make this work?"", B3-Ch12a-Manner, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Manner, v_b3_ch12_chance = 1)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Manner
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_chance == 0) {
"Will we be able to use the same strategy against the God of Time?"

"Of course not," Arraka says. "The God of Time manifests on the material plane as cohesive blue light. Have you ever tried encasing light in ice?"

"No," I admit. "Though I hadn't seen humanoid light before either."

"Light offers no physical mass to freeze," Arraka says. "Against the time god, we rely on sharpened element auras, Daren's blade, and Hadrik's hardened fists. Plus my personal matrices, if you ever manage to learn them."

"By the gods," Hadrik groans, leaning back against his stump. "You promised 'one last thing' half an hour ago. Can we have one evening without a strategic council?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_chance == 1) {
"So, I'm guessing that we will only have one chance to make this work?"

"Exactly once," Arraka says. "She won't expect an elemental burst of that scale from an unknown party, but once she sees it, her active defenses go up. We wait for a clean opening or we waste the trump card."

"By the gods," Hadrik groans, leaning back against his stump. "You promised 'one last thing' half an hour ago. Can we have one evening without a strategic council?"

}
"I am finished, you drunkard," Arraka snaps. "Drink your poison. I have research to resume."

"Music to my ears," Hadrik says, raising his bottle.

We spend another half-hour passing the bottle around the embers, steering clear of tactical talk, before Diane and I head toward the riverbank to test my cantrips as promised.

Walking along the gravel bar, I keep an eye on her. Her eagerness to volunteer stands out. Kate had an immediate, visceral reaction to the idea of spell testing; Diane treated it like an invitation to a parlor game.

It fits the pattern of the last two days. Ever since leaving the lessathi base, Diane has been working hard to ingratiate herself with the group. Caring about Kate is natural enough, but Diane is Meridith's second-in-command. Cultivating trust with the team carrying the tournament's wildcard pieces aligns neatly with lessathi diplomacy.

Daren and Hadrik have kept their distance, polite but guarded. I don't want to nurse unearned suspicion toward Kate's oldest friend, but excessive charm has a way of ringing false.

"This clearing should work," Diane says, stopping on a dry gravel spit twenty paces from the treeline. "Out of earshot so we don't disrupt the others. What are we testing, Barry? I have a high pain tolerance, so don't hold back."

choice(""I'm not sure if I should be relieved or worried that you feel comfortable joking about this."", B3-Ch12a-Describe, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Describe, v_b3_ch12_relieved = 0)
choice(""That's reassuring, because my next spell does have an unusually high mortality rate."", B3-Ch12a-Describe, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Describe, v_b3_ch12_relieved = 1)
choice(""I actually wasn't planning on using any painful spells, but that's good to know."", B3-Ch12a-Describe, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Describe, v_b3_ch12_relieved = 2)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Describe
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_relieved == 0) {
"I'm not sure if I should be relieved or worried that you feel comfortable joking about this," I say, dropping my pack to pull out the notes.

A sudden, light swat lands on the back of my head.

"When someone makes a dark joke about their past, standard etiquette is to match it," Diane says, hands on her hips. "Let's try that again."

"Could you repeat the joke?" I ask, rubbing the spot. "The blow gave me amnesia."

Diane snorts, breaking into a grin. "Not the retort I expected, but it earns you a pass for now."

"Deeply honored," I say with a dry bow.

"So—which matrix do we begin with?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_relieved == 1) {
"That's reassuring, because my next spell does have an unusually high mortality rate," I say, dropping my pack and unbuckling the notebook.

Diane gives a sharp laugh that cuts through the quiet of the riverbank.

"I didn't expect you to match the joke," she says. "The lessathi staff look at the floor whenever I bring up the past, and Kate treats every reference like an emergency. I appreciate someone who doesn't walk on eggshells."

"Happy to oblige," I say.

"So—which matrix do we begin with?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_relieved == 2) {
"I actually wasn't planning on using any painful spells, but that's good to know," I say, setting my pack down and opening the notebook.

"Nothing painful?" Diane asks. "I assumed this was combat preparation."

"At level one, direct offensive spells are useless," I say. "Sensory disruption and balance interference are the only cantrips that carry utility regardless of output."

"Sensible," Diane nods. "Where do we start?"

}
"The minor dizziness pulse from earlier," I say. "Tell me exactly what registers across the first few seconds. I need to know if the flow has distinct stages."

"I have a passive anti-dizziness ward active," Diane notes. "Do you want it dropped?"

"Keep it up," I say. "Most frontline fighters carry basic equilibrium wards. Let's see how the baseline registers."

I set my stance and loose the cantrip. The pulse meets her defensive aura and dissolves instantly, but Diane blinks twice, tilting her head.

"Interesting," she murmurs. "Was it supposed to produce a high ringing in the ears?"

"Ringing?" I flip a page. "That isn't in the standard notes for this cantrip. High pitch? Pulsing in half-second intervals?"

"Exactly that," Diane says. "How did you know?"

"The harmonic flow borrows its geometry from Wolford's daze," I say, tapping the diagram. "I didn't expect the auditory artifact to bleed through an active shield. If the sound registers even when the spell fails, I can use it as a bluff—make an enemy assume a full Wolford lock is coming."

"Drop the ward for round two?" Diane asks.

"One more against the shield to confirm the resonance, then we test clean."

"Whenever you're ready."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Nausea, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Nausea)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Nausea
TEXT:

We work along the gravel bar for thirty minutes, cataloguing reactions across half a dozen utility cantrips. Without her defensive ward, the dizziness spell leaves her blinking through a mild headache for three seconds before clearing. We run basic visual decoys, a low-tier suggestion thread, and two sensory-scrambling cantrips.

Through every test, Diane follows instructions without complaint. Her demeanor remains light and engaging, though reading what sits behind it is difficult. Unlike Kate, whose feelings surface as blunt irritation or sharp silences, Diane smooths over every edge with humor and polite composure.

Yet beneath that diplomatic layer lies an impulsive streak. During the God of Time's memory trials, the younger Diane was fiercely direct, even reckless. The polished poise feels like Meridith's training—an acquired armor she wears to keep control.

"That wraps the index for tonight," I say, closing the notebook. "Thanks for the target practice."

"Finished already?" Diane stretches her arms. "I was just getting used to the vertigo."

"If you miss the nausea, Hadrik has two barrels of dwarven stout," I say. "One cup will do the job."

Diane's smile fades slightly. "I don't think Hadrik is looking to share his ale with me."

"Why? Did something happen?"

"Every conversation feels strained," Diane says, looking toward the campfires. "He answers in short sentences and looks for the nearest exit. I don't blame him—I left him unconscious in the sacred woods during our first encounter. But trying to smooth things over hasn't made much headway. Do you know what might break the ice? A favorite game? A topic he cares about?"

choice("Tell Diane that Hadrik likes to play Met-Zek.", B3-Ch12a-Advice, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Advice, v_b3_ch12_diane = 0)
choice("Tell Diane that she should try less hard to be friends with everyone.", B3-Ch12a-Advice, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Advice, v_b3_ch12_diane = 1)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Advice
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_diane == 1) {
"Honestly," I say, "Hadrik's guard is probably up because you're pushing too hard. Forcing friendliness makes people look for the angle. Give him room to breathe. You don't need a clean friendship after forty-eight hours on the road."

Diane considers this for a moment, then nods slowly. "You might have a point. Overcompensating for a bad first impression rarely helps. I'll step back and let things settle."

"Good plan," I say. "And if you ever need a warm, patient conversationalist, Arraka is always waiting in the amulet."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_diane == 0) {
"He plays Met-Zek," I tell her. "Challenge him to a hand. He never turns down a match."

"Met-Zek," Diane muses. "It's been years, but the rules are simple enough. I'll give it a shot."

"Good luck," I say. "And if Hadrik remains stubborn, there's always Arraka. She is famously known for her gentle listening ear."

}
Diane laughs. "I might take you up on that right now. Thanks, Barry."

She heads off toward Flower's tent, leaving me on the riverbank.

I shoulder my pack and walk back toward the boulders to practice a few combinations before watch duty. Near the edge of the pines, Kate is running her aim drills, casting icicles at marked leaves in the canopy.

Something about her rhythm is off. Usually, Kate conjures her shards from unpredictable angles—behind her shoulder, from the grass, hovering at head height—to test her control. Today, every icicle forms in the exact same spot two feet in front of her chest. Her gaze is vacant, fixed on the branches without really seeing them, her hands repeating the motion on pure instinct.

It is the third time in two days I've seen her drift through practice.

choice("Go talk to Kate.", B3-Ch12a-Kate1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Kate1, v_b3_ch12_kate = 1)
choice("Ignore Kate and go test some spell combinations.", B3-Ch12a-Ignore, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Ignore, v_b3_ch12_kate = 0)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Kate1
TEXT:

"Evening, Kate," I say, stepping onto the grass near her target line. "How are the drills?"

"Same as usual," Kate says without looking over, sending two more icicles whistling through a cluster of high oak leaves. "Why?"

"Same as usual?" I lean against a pine. "Since when does your target routine involve conjuring every shard from the exact same point in front of your breastbone?"

Kate's fingers twitch, about to manifest another needle from the same anchor, then she halts. She sweeps her hand wide; three shards condense along different axes and shatter against the high branches.

"I didn't realize I was doing that," she murmurs, lowering her arm. "How long have you been watching?"

"Yesterday afternoon was the first time," I say. "Three sessions now."

Kate looks out into the dark treeline, silent for a moment.

"So what's on your mind?" I ask. "You don't drift through aim practice unless something is chewing on you. Did Diane say something?"

"It's nothing," Kate says, looking away. "Nothing you need to fix."

"You came back to Varathia to pull Diane out," I say quietly. "Only Diane doesn't want to leave—and staying with her means signing on with Meridith."

Kate exhales slowly, her shoulders dropping a fraction.

"That's only part of it," she says. "It isn't just what happens when the tournament ends. Diane feels... different. I didn't expect ten years to vanish, but talking to her now is like speaking through a screen. Reconnecting with Leila was effortless, even though we barely spoke at the Beacon. With Diane, it feels like we're strangers performing a script."

"You aren't the only one feeling the wall," I tell her. "Hadrik and Daren keep their distance too. Her charm is too practiced. It feels like talking to the king of the underground."

Kate turns to me, her dark eyes sharp. "The king of the underground. That's the rhythm. The cadence. The way she shields every reaction." She rubs her temple. "I knew there was an echo I couldn't place."

"So that explains the stiffness," I say. "What about Galamir? When the tournament wraps and Diane stays with the lessathi?"

Kate looks down at her boots. "I've been putting that decision off. We aren't in Galamir yet. I don't hate Meridith, but building a life under lessathi authority is the last thing I want."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Kate2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Kate2)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Kate2
TEXT:

"What if you negotiate a travel dispensation?" I suggest. "Meridith runs the surface operations. She could grant you an open passage into Varathia whenever you want to visit. You wouldn't be tied to their faction, and you wouldn't be cutting ties with Diane."

Kate considers that, her expression softening. "An open pass... That might actually work. I'll bring it up with Diane when the topic comes up."

She hesitates, looking across the quiet clearing.

"Thank you, Barry," she says quietly. "I know I can be difficult to deal with. I appreciate that you stay."

"You handed me the breakthrough that kept me in this tournament," I say. "Without your Magium-device theory, I'd still be an ordinary human with an empty notebook. I don't forget debts."

A faint, genuine smile touches the corners of her mouth. "Good."

"Barry!" Daren's shout carries from the camp perimeter. "Shift change! You ready?"

"On my way!" I call back, then turn to Kate. "Gotta run."

"Go," Kate says. "I'll finish this set and turn in."

"Good night, Kate."

"Good night."

She raises her hand, conjuring three shards from wide, shifting angles, her focus locked on the leaves above.

I shoulder my pack and head toward the watch post.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Sky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Sky)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Ignore
TEXT:

Better to let her have the clearing. When Kate needs space, crowding her only raises more ice.

I skirt the boulder line and find a dry hollow fifty yards out, away from the tents. After three misfires on complex weaves, I dial the ambition back to basics.

Balancing fire and water channels in equal measures produces a dense, localized bank of warm steam. It won't fool anyone tracking auras, but in low light, it cuts line-of-sight fast enough to break an archer's aim.

At level one, every small edge counts.

"Barry!" Daren's shout echoes through the trees. "Shift change! You ready?"

"On my way!" I call out, dispersing the steam cloud.

I pack the notes and head toward our agreed watch point.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Sky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Sky)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Sky
TEXT:

On my way to the perimeter, I pass Flower's tent and catch Diane in conversation with Arraka. They have been speaking at least once a day since the underground complex, trading notes in tones that sound almost polite.

"What is the magical plane actually like?" Diane asks, leaning against the guy-rope. "I hear the lectures, but without a physical frame of reference, it's hard to picture."

"You are an ethereal, yes?" Arraka says from the pendant. "The magical plane is structurally similar to the ether, only instead of a sea of light, it is an ocean of raw, unshaped force. Dense currents of energy with no matter to anchor them."

"That helps," Diane says, nodding. "Thank you. It's late—I should turn in."

"Certainly," Arraka says in a voice dripping with theatrical sweetness. "One final question before bed. What color is the sky tonight?"

Diane looks up at the ink-black canopy and smiles. "Bright pink, of course. Just like every evening."

"Much obliged," Arraka purrs.

Diane offers a neat bow and heads toward her bedroll.

I step forward as the tent flap settles. "You two seem remarkably cordial."

"The girl is delightful," Arraka says. "I close every exchange with that exact question. She gives the exact same lie, and her aura registers a completely different signature every time."

"How?"

"She runs the same concealment technique the king of the underground used," Arraka explains. "Meridith clearly drilled her in it. Most lessathi use a crude dampener, but Diane introduces deliberate micro-tremors at random intervals. Her aura quivers whether she speaks truth or falsehood. Total noise."

"A counter against Eiden?" I ask.

"Obviously," Arraka says. "He reads deception by aura spikes. If the entire field is erratic, his tell vanishes. It has been centuries since anyone presented me with a puzzle I couldn't crack in ten seconds. I am savoring the challenge."

"So you haven't broken the pattern yet?"

"She will slip," Arraka says confidently. "No discipline is flawless."

choice(""I think you're delusional. If you haven't beaten her by now you don't stand a chance."", B3-Ch12a-Stories, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Stories, v_b3_ch12_delusional = 0)
choice(""So, what's your plan to win this game, then?"", B3-Ch12a-Stories, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Stories, v_b3_ch12_delusional = 1)
choice(""Have you tried making her lose control over her emotions?"", B3-Ch12a-Stories, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Stories, v_b3_ch12_delusional = 2)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Stories
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch12_battle,1) if (v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2)
achievement("Preparing for the big battle",v_ac_b3_ch12_battle)
#if(v_b3_ch12_delusional == 0) {
"I think you're delusional. If you haven't beaten her by now you don't stand a chance."

"Perhaps," Arraka says mildly. "Perhaps not."

"That is an uncharacteristically calm response," I note.

"It is a pastime," Arraka says. "Whether she tells the truth about the sky is irrelevant. The mechanics interest me, not the answers."

"Fair enough. I have watch duty."

"Go, mortal."

"See you in the morning, Barry!" Flower chirps from within.

I wave toward the tent and head into the dark woods toward the watch post.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_delusional == 1) {
"So, what's your plan to win this game, then?"

"Observation," Arraka says. "I catalogue the tremor frequencies with every pass. She is young and overconfident. To her, I am merely a captive voice in a trinket; she doesn't treat me with the caution a five-thousand-year-old archmage warrants. A lapse in concentration is inevitable."

"Good luck with that," I say. "I have watch duty."

"Run along, Barry-boy."

"See you in the morning, Barry!" Flower calls out.

I wave toward the tent and head into the dark woods toward the watch post.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_delusional == 2) {
"Have you tried making her lose control over her emotions?"

"That ruins the dataset," Arraka says. "The technique relies on controlled randomness. When she is calm, I know the tremors are artificial, which allows me to isolate the underlying cadence. If she panics, genuine emotional turbulence contaminates the signal."

"Understood. Enjoy the puzzle," I say. "I have watch duty."

"Mhm."

"See you in the morning, Barry!" Flower calls out.

I wave toward the tent and head into the dark woods toward the watch post.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2) {
I settle against a broad pine at the edge of the clearing, opening my notebook. Weaving spells so close to camp is asking for trouble, so I stick to optical theory.

Twenty minutes later, Daren emerges from the brush, holding up a worn deck of cards with an apologetic grin. Hadrik cornered him for a quick hand of Met-Zek on the way out.

"Researching another formula?" Daren asks, nodding at my sketches.

"Drafting the schematics for attaching a hard-light mustache to the God of Time's jawline," I say. "Real bristles or pure optical illusion?"

"How about neither?" Daren rolls his eyes. "We agreed not to antagonize a deity until we have a viable battle plan. Do you want another temporal rewind?"

"Relax," I say. "The time god doesn't even know what facial hair is. He'd probably ignore it."

"Comforting thought," Daren mutters, shuffling the deck.

For the next few hours, we trade deals and play regional card games under the canopy. Daren usually carries the conversation with tales from his eastern campaigns, but tonight he turns the questions back on me, pressing for details about my years roaming the Western Continent.

I warn him that odd jobs and library scraps make poor bardic material compared to monster hunts, but as I talk through old escorts, ruined towers, and failed experiments, Daren leans forward, genuinely hooked on every turn.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 0 && v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
I settle against a broad pine at the edge of the clearing, opening my notebook. Weaving spells so close to camp is asking for trouble, so I stick to summoning schematics.

Twenty minutes later, Daren emerges from the brush, holding up a worn deck of cards with an apologetic grin. Hadrik cornered him for a quick hand of Met-Zek on the way out.

"Researching another formula?" Daren asks, glancing at the diagrams.

"Mapping the spatial anchor for the toad conjuration," I say. "I need an ether pass to check the mana draw, but the particle path looks clean."

"A long road ahead," Daren notes.

"An hour or two of practice, at most," I say. "Ready for cards?"

"Deal them out," Daren says, sitting opposite me on the moss.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 1 && v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
I settle against a broad pine at the edge of the clearing, opening my notebook. Weaving spells so close to camp is asking for trouble, so I stick to kinetic schematics.

Twenty minutes later, Daren emerges from the brush, holding up a worn deck of cards with an apologetic grin. Hadrik cornered him for a quick hand of Met-Zek on the way out.

"Researching another formula?" Daren asks, glancing at the diagrams.

"Mapping the suspension lattice for the floating dagger," I say. "I need an ether pass to check the balance, but the particle path looks clean."

"A long road ahead," Daren notes.

"An hour or two of practice, at most," I say. "Ready for cards?"

"Deal them out," Daren says, sitting opposite me on the moss.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 2 && v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
I settle against a broad pine at the edge of the clearing, opening my notebook. Weaving spells so close to camp is asking for trouble, so I stick to optical schematics.

Twenty minutes later, Daren emerges from the brush, holding up a worn deck of cards with an apologetic grin. Hadrik cornered him for a quick hand of Met-Zek on the way out.

"Researching another formula?" Daren asks, glancing at the diagrams.

"Mapping the dispersion rate for the sparkler cantrip," I say. "I need an ether pass to check the burst radius, but the particle path looks clean."

"A long road ahead," Daren notes.

"An hour or two of practice, at most," I say. "Ready for cards?"

"Deal them out," Daren says, sitting opposite me on the moss.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_spell == 3 && v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
I settle against a broad pine at the edge of the clearing, opening my notebook. Weaving spells so close to camp is asking for trouble, so I stick to optical illusion schematics.

Twenty minutes later, Daren emerges from the brush, holding up a worn deck of cards with an apologetic grin. Hadrik cornered him for a quick hand of Met-Zek on the way out.

"Researching another formula?" Daren asks, glancing at the diagrams.

"Mapping the geometric mesh for Arraka's dunce cap," I say. "I need an ether pass to calibrate the hard-light anchor, but the particle path looks clean."

"A long road ahead," Daren notes.

"An hour or two of practice, at most," I say. "Ready for cards?"

"Deal them out," Daren says, sitting opposite me on the moss.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
For the next few hours, we trade deals and play regional card games under the canopy. Daren usually carries the conversation with tales from his eastern campaigns, but tonight he turns the questions back on me, pressing for details about my years roaming the Western Continent.

I warn him that odd jobs and library scraps make poor bardic material compared to monster hunts, but as I talk through old escorts, ruined towers, and failed experiments, Daren leans forward, genuinely hooked on every turn.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Brinhorn, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Brinhorn)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Brinhorn
TEXT:

"Leila was right," Daren says, gathering the cards as the night deepens. "You have been holding out on us. How many more of these stories are you sitting on?"

"None of it is heroic," I say, leaning back against the trunk. "Odd jobs, caravan guard shifts, translation work to keep food on the table while chasing research leads. Nobody writes songs about funding a library habit."

"They ought to," Daren laughs. "You kept me on the edge of my seat. I can't decide if the people you ran with were mad before meeting you or if your obsession infected them. Brinhorn, especially. The man always struck me as level-headed. How did you talk him into that research?"

"Brinhorn spends every winter winning the Western regional tournaments just to challenge Azarius for the sage title, losing every single time, and coming back the next year," I say. "If that isn't stubborn madness, what is? We were kindred spirits. I am surprised we haven't crossed paths with him here. With Azarius in the tournament, Brinhorn wouldn't stay home."

"Did you keep in touch after leaving the continent?" Daren asks.

"No," I say. "It's been years. I kept telling myself I would reach out once I actually found a way to cast, instead of sending another letter reporting dead ends. I wonder what he'd say seeing me throw sparks now."

"He'd be proud," Daren says warmly. "And motivated. If a non-mage can break the barrier, dethroning Azarius must look almost reasonable."

"Something like that," I say.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Humanity, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Humanity)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Humanity
TEXT:

"Speaking of impossible targets," Daren says quietly, leaning against his knees. "Have you given more thought to Meridith's offer? About Eiden?"

"The less we debate it aloud, the better," I say. "As long as our stance remains undecided, Eiden has no reason to treat us as an immediate threat."

"I know the tactical line," Daren says, looking down at his palms. "I mean privately. Have you thought about what we actually do if the choice lands in front of us?"

"The theory is simple enough," I say. "Why?"

"This sounds strange from me," Daren admits, his voice dropping. "Given what I said in Thilias. But after the God of Time's memory trial... I am not sure I can execute him in cold blood."

"The Eiden in our world has no memory of that trial, Daren."

"The man is the same," Daren insists, looking up. "The soul is identical. If pushed into that exact corner, our Eiden would break the same way. He looked me in the eye and said prove me wrong, Barry. What if part of him is still waiting for someone to give him a reason to care about humanity again?"

choice(""Didn't he already destroy an entire city? I'd say it's a little late for regrets."", B3-Ch12a-Inevitable, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Inevitable, v_b3_ch12_conflicted = 0)
choice(""Wow, you were right. This does sound weird, coming from you."", B3-Ch12a-Inevitable, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Inevitable, v_b3_ch12_conflicted = 1)
choice(""Actually, now that I think about it, I suppose I'm a little bit conflicted too."", B3-Ch12a-Inevitable, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Inevitable, v_b3_ch12_conflicted = 2)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Inevitable
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_conflicted == 0) {
"Didn't he already destroy an entire city? I'd say it's a little late for regrets."

"I don't excuse what he did," Daren says. "Olmnar is a massacre. But if that power could be turned back toward protecting people, think of what he could rebuild. Execution ends the threat, but it also buries the only force capable of restoring this continent."

"Assuming he doesn't lapse into apathy five years later," I say.

"While we breathe, we can hold him to his word," Daren says. "Letting that power rot in cynicism feels like a failure of its own."

"Regrets don't erase the prophecy," I point out. "Eiden told us his clash will tear Varathia's cities apart. The kings and lessathi aren't plotting out of spite—they are trying to survive."

"If the catalyst is his war with Tyrath," Daren says, "wouldn't removing Tyrath solve the equation?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_conflicted == 1) {
"Wow, you were right. This does sound weird, coming from you."

"I never imagined defending him either," Daren says with a dry grimace. "In Thilias, I wanted him dead where he stood. Now I look at the scale of his magic and wonder what happens if he remembers who he was. A Creator working for the people again."

"His first attempt ended in disaster," I tell him.

"Because he carried the burden alone," Daren says. "If he wants to be proven wrong, that is an opening. Not a guarantee, but an opening."

"The kings of Varathia won't see it as an opening," I say. "They know the prophecy from Meridith. They expect their capitals to burn, and they want him eliminated before the spark catches."

"If the spark is Tyrath," Daren asks, "why not focus our blades on the dragon?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_conflicted == 2) {
"Actually, now that I think about it, I suppose I'm a little bit conflicted too," I say. "Eiden gave us room to operate. If he wanted us dead, we wouldn't have survived the forest clearing. He is testing us."

"Exactly," Daren says, leaning in. "Think of the good that power could do if steered in the right direction."

"Maybe," I say. "Though the political reality is ugly. Every crowned head on this continent wants him dead because of the prophecy. Even if Eiden decided to put the crown back on, the cities wouldn't welcome him."

"If the threat to the cities comes from his duel with Tyrath," Daren says, "wouldn't killing the dragon neutralize the danger?"

}
"After what Meridith showed us," I say, "I doubt the 'two old enemies' from the prophecy are Eiden and Tyrath. The real war is Eiden versus the lessathi. Melindra claims he sided with them during the Still Winter, but Meridith is preparing for an existential showdown. That clash is what levels the cities."

"Why would the Magium deliver a prophecy about a war its own fragment is actively orchestrating?" Daren rubs his brow. "It makes no sense."

"Arraka says the Magium and its localized fragments are separate entities," I explain. "The fragment in the lessathi node has been directing Meridith for months. Its priority isn't regional peace; it wants Eiden erased. It might be the same fragment that handed Eiden the secrets to bind Arraka centuries ago, now deciding its chosen instrument has outlived its usefulness."

"Then why doesn't the fragment erase him directly?" Daren asks. "Why contract mortals?"

"Spatial constraints," I say. "Every erasure we witnessed occurred inside the Magium realm or inches from an awakened crystal node. Outside its direct domain, a fragment lacks surgical reach. When it targeted past-Arraka, it threatened to unravel the entire dimensional fabric just to locate her. It uses local factions because it cannot drop a lightning bolt from the sky with pinpoint precision."

Daren sighs, rubbing his temples. "I'm lost, Barry. Nodes, fragments, cosmic politics—it's too many layers. All I want is clean access to a node to pull healing lore and leave the rest buried. No collateral damage."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Unusual, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Unusual)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Unusual
TEXT:

"Clean access requires an untethered node," I tell him. "The tournament organizers won't hand us a key, so our options are Meridith's bargain or locating an independent tap. At least now we know the nodes are physical and functional. That's more than we had a week ago."

"True enough," Daren says, standing up to stretch his shoulders. "We ask Arraka and Illuna about other node locations in the morning. They've tapped them before."

"Agreed. Let's wake Kate and Diane for the second watch."

We pack the cards, swap shifts at the perimeter, and catch four hours of unbroken sleep.

Dawn arrives with the rich, savory aroma of roasted wyvern. For an ordinary tavern, wyvern steak is a king's banquet; Flower manifests platters of it over her fire pit without breaking a sweat.

As the camp stirs, Daren finishes packing his bedroll while speaking into his transceiver. He checks in with Sophie three times a day now, catching up on years of missed quiet. She has already adapted to our traveling rhythm over the line, trading jokes with Hadrik and planning to join the group once the tournament dust settles.

Between bites of wyvern, the camp air is full of voices. Leila's father relays her written messages over Daren's spare frequency, reading out her tactical notes in his gruff voice. Flower calls Melindra every hour on the hour, shouting updates about cloud formations and trail markers, while Kate occasionally slips behind the trees with her own transceiver to check on the stillwater in private.

Across the magical plane, Jirinn remains in frequent contact with Arraka. According to the spirit, word of the gods' illicit soul-absorption has shattered the celestial hierarchy. Rebel factions are joining Jirinn's enlightened warriors in force, though the rogue gods—Time, Death, and Fate—remain entrenched behind Fate's layered illusions. Every day they spend fighting on the higher plane is a day the God of Time is too distracted to hunt us on the ground.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Fyron, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Fyron)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Fyron
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
Rose calls on the transceiver during our morning briefing. Arraka tracks every fraction of progress in her temporal control. Rose can hold a worldwide time-stop for fifteen seconds before exhaustion sets in, keeping us anchored and unfrozen across the distance as long as she has our voices over the line. Her localized time-dilation is coming along, though that effect only covers a narrow radius around her in Thilias.

"Illuna," I say as we sit down to eat. "About the Magium nodes."

"If this is about the char on the meat," Illuna says, "speak to Flower."

"Not the meat," I say. "You and Arraka accessed an earthen node before. Is the location something you can share?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Illuna," I say as we sit down to eat. "About the Magium nodes. You and Arraka accessed an earthen node before. Is the location something you can share?"

"If this is about the char on the meat," Illuna says, "speak to Flower."

"Not the meat," I say. "The node you used in the past. Can you tell us where it is?"

}
"Fyron's node," Illuna says, setting down her skewer. "Arraka used it centuries ago, though she never saw fit to mention it during my apprenticeship."

"Why would I?" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "I pulled the essentials from the Magium on my own plane. The earthen nodes were useless for finding a passage home. Being bound to stone or beast bodies limited my field research."

"How did you negotiate access with Fyron?" Daren asks.

"Minor favors," Illuna says. "A scouting run, an escort, a ward. That is Fyron's currency. When we reach Galamir for the war summit, you can negotiate your own terms."

"He will be there for the alliance talks against Tyrath," Daren says. "We can catch him before the delegations convene."

"The talks won't start without the full roster," Diane adds with a light shrug. "Meridith mentioned half the representatives are running late. You'll have time."

"Shouldn't that concern you?" Illuna raises an eyebrow. "If we secure Fyron's node, Meridith loses her leverage to buy an assassination on Eiden."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Obscure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Obscure)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Obscure
TEXT:

"I have no stake in the assassination contract," Diane says easily. "If you refuse her offer, Kate doesn't have to face Eiden in a death match. Keep that between us, but I prefer Kate breathing."

"That sentiment would carry more weight," Illuna says coldly, "if your aura weren't constantly masked."

"I don't hold the dampener for sport," Diane replies. "An aura mask has to be active habit. If I drop it, raising it fast enough to fool Eiden's instant teleportation is impossible. I can't afford the lag."

"Convenient," Illuna mutters. "Since no one else here runs that matrix, we take your word for it."

"I know we have only traveled together two days," Diane says, looking around the circle. "You have no reason to trust me beyond Kate's word. I am sorry if the precaution causes discomfort."

"I felt the distance when we met," Kate says, stepping in. "Now I know what I was sensing. If any caster here had the discipline to stay immune to lie-detection, they would run it constantly. Wouldn't you?"

"Without question," Hadrik grunts around a mouthful of wyvern.

"Fair enough," Daren concedes.

Diane smiles at Kate. "Thank you."

choice(""Actually, I would never use such a technique even if I had it!"", B3-Ch12a-Plates, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Plates, v_b3_ch12_sincere = 1)
choice(""I agree with Kate. There would be nothing wrong with you wanting some privacy."", B3-Ch12a-Plates, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Plates, v_b3_ch12_sincere = 0)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Plates
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_sincere,2) if (v_b3_ch12_sincere == 1 && v_bluff == 0)
#if(v_b3_ch12_sincere == 1 && v_bluff > 0) {
"Actually, I would never touch a technique like that," I say.

"Of course not," Hadrik chuckles. "You rely on the bluff stat in your gadget instead."

"The device provides organic deception," I say. "Far more artisanal."

"Is that so?" Diane's eyes gleam. "We should hold a contest. See whose technique holds up under pressure."

"Name the time," I tell her.

}
#if(v_bluff == 0 && v_b3_ch12_sincere != 0 || v_b3_ch12_sincere == 2) {
"Actually, I would never touch a technique like that," I say.

"Because you have a bluff stat you haven't invested in yet," Hadrik snorts.

"I am an open book," I say. "Sincerity is my core discipline."

"Your core discipline?" Diane asks, amused.

"Barry 'Sincere' the Reckless," I say. "Ask anyone."

"Noted, Mr. Reckless," Diane says with a grin.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_sincere == 0) {
"I agree with Kate," I say. "Privacy is a reasonable survival habit."

"I appreciate the understanding," Diane says. "I know an obscured aura breeds suspicion. I am trying to keep my actions transparent."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_sincere == 0 || v_b3_ch12_sincere == 2 || v_bluff >= 0) {
"Can I wash the dishes?" Flower asks, gathering empty skewers.

"I have the dishes," Illuna says, taking the body. "I adapted the matrix we pulled from the lessathi node, and this is a controlled drill."

"Magium lore for washing grease off tin?" Hadrik blinks.

"It is a combat matrix," Illuna says, lifting a tin plate into the air inside a churning sphere of water.

"Hold on," Arraka's voice rises. "That is my drowning sphere! The construct you used on Tyrath in Thilias!"

"It is an upgraded iteration," Illuna says. "The lattice is distinct enough to be my own."

"Touching," Arraka sneers. "You finally put your resentment aside long enough to cast my curriculum."

"If you have critiques on the flow, offer them," Illuna says. "I reinforced the outer shell and increased the replenishment rate to withstand continuous fire."

"Competent," Arraka admits grudgingly. "A few nodes on the lower arc are sloppy. Adjust the intake here and here."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Sober, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Sober) if (v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1 && v_b3_ch12_sincere == 0 || v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1 && v_b3_ch12_sincere == 2 || v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1 && v_bluff == 0 || v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1 && v_bluff > 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Rock, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Rock) if (v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0 && v_b3_ch12_sincere == 0 || v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0 && v_b3_ch12_sincere == 2 || v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0 && v_bluff == 0 || v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0 && v_bluff > 0)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Sober
TEXT:

"No ale this morning, Hadrik?" Daren asks, glancing over while Illuna adjusts the water sphere. "That's a first."

"Don't remind me," Hadrik grunts, rubbing his temples with a grimace. "Headache since sunrise."

"Special occasion?"

"Barry's special occasion," Hadrik mutters. "His dizziness cantrip triggered an old reaction. Certain disorientation spells leave me with a day-long nausea whenever I smell alcohol. The sensitivity peaks the morning after and lingers until dusk."

"My fault," I say. "I would have picked another cantrip if I knew."

"No harm done, lad. Never mentioned it," Hadrik says, rolling his shoulders. "Daren, since I can't drink, take up your sword. Spar with me until Illuna finishes the plates. I need to burn the rust off."

"Gladly," Daren says, drawing his practice blade.

They step thirty paces into the open grass, square their stances, and engage on three.

Thirty seconds in, Daren leaps backward, holding up a flat palm.

"Hold! Stop!" Daren calls out, staring at the dwarf. "Hadrik, what are you doing?"

"Fighting," Hadrik says, bouncing lightly on his boots. "Same as every morning."

"Not like this," Daren breathes, eyes wide. "Your cadence is completely different. The footwork, the balance—I couldn't read your aura at all. It was like sparring a stranger."

"Oh, right," Hadrik rumbles, stroking his beard. "People mentioned in the past that I fight sharper when sober. It's been so long since I took a day off that I forgot."

"Hadrik, that wasn't just sharper," Daren says earnestly. "That was extraordinary. If you fight like this without ale, why not stay dry until the tournament ends? You'd be unstoppable."

Hadrik gives him a long, pitying look. "Daren, my friend... Drinking is like breathing. If someone told you you'd fight ten times better if you stopped breathing air, would you hold your breath?"

Daren stares at him, sighs deeply, and sheathes his blade. "I don't know why I asked. Illuna, are the plates finished?"

"Last one," Illuna calls out.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12a-Rock, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12a-Rock)


ID: B3-Ch12a-Rock
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_diane == 1 && v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0) {
While Illuna finishes the dishes, we strike the tents, pack the bedrolls, and stow the tins in Hadrik's pack. Ten minutes later, we are on the march, heading toward the border of the sacred woods.

According to Diane, we should reach the perimeter by mid-afternoon. We have no formal plan if the golden fox intercepts us again over pinecones, but with our current roster of enemies, adding one more legendary beast barely moves the needle.

On the trail, Diane settles into an easier pace, no longer forcing conversation. Giving the others space pays immediate dividends—Hadrik and Daren trade quiet trail banter with her without the defensive edge from before.

"Look!" Flower gasps, darting off the path toward a gray stone in the moss. "Barry, do you see it? A cloakshield rock! A genuine cloakshield rock!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_diane == 0 && v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0) {
While Illuna finishes the dishes, we strike the tents, pack the bedrolls, and stow the tins in Hadrik's pack. Ten minutes later, we are on the march, heading toward the border of the sacred woods.

According to Diane, we should reach the perimeter by mid-afternoon. We have no formal plan if the golden fox intercepts us again over pinecones, but with our current roster of enemies, adding one more legendary beast barely moves the needle.

On the trail, Diane continues to push, trying hard to engage everyone in conversation. The forced cheer only deepens the reserve between her and the frontline fighters; Daren and Hadrik answer in clipped syllables, their guards firmly up.

"Look!" Flower gasps, darting off the path toward a gray stone in the moss. "Barry, do you see it? A cloakshield rock! A genuine cloakshield rock!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_diane == 1 && v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1) {
Once Illuna packs the clean tins into Hadrik's pack, we finish dismantling the camp and take to the trail, heading toward the sacred forest.

Diane estimates an afternoon arrival. Whether the golden fox takes offense at another border trespass remains an open question, but among stillwaters, gods, and rogue dragons, another territorial guardian is just routine transit.

On the march, Diane maintains a relaxed distance, speaking when spoken to. Without the forced enthusiasm, Hadrik and Daren ease up, trading occasional comments with her about trail conditions and terrain.

"Look!" Flower gasps, darting off the path toward a gray stone in the moss. "Barry, do you see it? A cloakshield rock! A genuine cloakshield rock!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_diane == 0 && v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1) {
Once Illuna packs the clean tins into Hadrik's pack, we finish dismantling the camp and take to the trail, heading toward the sacred forest.

Diane estimates an afternoon arrival. Whether the golden fox takes offense at another border trespass remains an open question, but among stillwaters, gods, and rogue dragons, another territorial guardian is just routine transit.

On the march, Diane keeps pressing to be involved in every exchange, trying to compensate for the morning's distance. The relentless effort only tightens Hadrik and Daren's defenses, keeping every conversation stiff and formal.

"Look!" Flower gasps, darting off the path toward a gray stone in the moss. "Barry, do you see it? A cloakshield rock! A genuine cloakshield rock!"

}
"I see it," I say without enthusiasm.

"That stone resembles neither a cloak nor a shield," Daren points out. "Why the name?"

Flower gives him a look of profound disappointment, as though pitying a man blind to high art. "You wouldn't understand."

She reaches down for the stone—and passes straight through an invisible ripple in the air.

"By the void!" Arraka's voice shrills from the chest piece. "Not another one!"

"Plenty of rocks on the earthen plane," Hadrik grunts. "Get used to them."

"Not the rock, you buffoon!" Arraka snaps. "One of Eiden's invisible surveillance wards! The child walked right through the tripwire, and the resonance gives this casing a dreadful itch."

"Another one?" Daren asks, pausing. "How many has she triggered?"

"Five in two days," Arraka says. "And those are just the ones Flower wandered into by accident. Eiden has been weaving a continental grid."

"Do you know the matrix purpose?" I ask.

"No," Arraka says. "I could decode the harmonic structure, but you would have to camp in place for twenty-four hours while I dismantle the weave. A waste of my time."

"Shame," Daren says. "If Eiden is blanketing the continent in invisible wards, understanding his objective might show us where he stands."

"Here is a thought," Arraka sneers. "How about we don't pick a fight with Eiden while our schedule is already booked with god-slaying and past-erasure? One crisis at a time."

"I was looking for common ground, not a fight," Daren mutters.

"Certainly you were," Arraka says. "Keep marching. Nothing to see."

Ahead on the trail, Flower pulls her transceiver from her sash and speaks loudly into the horn.

"Melindra! You will never guess what rock I found in the grass!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Distracting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Distracting)
